  data:  # dd32
    kind: invariant  # dd32
    title: "cpp emits call_construct on ZERO call edges, so the F3 method gate is unconditional for C++ (2 of 59 measured FPs)"  # dd32
    status: violated  # dd32
    priority: 1  # dd32
    parent:  # dd32
    tags:  # dd32
    - io_boundary  # dd32
    - cpp  # dd32
    - analyzer  # dd32
    - security  # dd32
    isbefore: []  # dd32
    duplicate_of: []  # dd32
    not_duplicate_of: []  # dd32
    pr_ref:  # dd32
    description: "FOUND while measuring INV-funuf's false-positive rate, which is the only\nreason it has a number attached rather than being a suspicion.\n\nThe cpp analyzer emits no call_construct on any call edge. Read off a real\nbehavior map of whisper.cpp, every cpp call edge's meta is exactly:\n\n  {\"evidence_lang\": \"cpp\", \"evidence_type\": \"ast_call_direct\"}\n\nfor BOTH a free-function call and a method call on a C++ object. So the\nio-boundary F3 gate's method rule - \"an untyped method call cannot be\nverified against the catalogued receiver type, so it never matches\", which\nis what closes INV-tapat and INV-maluk - is unconditional for C++.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE, on the merged INV-funuf tree: 2 of 59 newly-matched\ncpp boundaries are false, and both have the same shape. In\nggml/src/ggml-vulkan/vulkan-shaders/vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp:\n\n  compile_count_cond.wait(guard, ...)   -> matched sys/wait.wait [subprocess]\n  c.wait()                              -> matched sys/wait.wait [subprocess]\n\nThose are std::condition_variable::wait and std::future::wait. The file\ngenuinely includes <sys/wait.h> and genuinely calls waitpid elsewhere (a\nTRUE positive), so the module evidence is correct and the ONLY thing that\nseparates the real POSIX wait() from the two C++ methods is the call\nconstruct.\n\nWHY NOT FIXED IN INV-funuf: nothing to gate on. Wiring the guard while the\nproducer emits nothing would be a predicate with no call site passing it -\ninert by construction, and this repo has paid for that shape before\n(io_mode stamped at one site; the F3 arm skipping select_by_mode). The fix\nbelongs at the producer.\n\nSAME CLASS, ALREADY FILED FOR OTHER LANGUAGES - this is the third instance,\nwhich is the threshold at which this project files the class rather than\nthe instance:\n  INV-gijis  JS/TS populates call_construct on ZERO call edges (4,872\n             measured across 3 repos), second-largest sink surface.\n  INV-suril  java.nio.file.Files static methods catalogued kind=method but\n             emitted with no call_construct, so the whole Files sink\n             surface can never match.\nWhether these three want one shared producer-side rule plus a parity test\nover every analyzer, or three fixes, is the question to answer FIRST -\npicking it off per-language is what produced three items.\n\nREPRO: analyze any C++ repo with a method call whose name collides with a\nPOSIX function in an included header; observe io_boundary tagged from the\nfunction-kind catalogue row. The whisper.cpp sites above are the filed\ninstance."  # dd32
    fields:  # dd32
      statement: "A call edge must carry the construct it was called with, so a catalogued receiver type can be verified. VIOLATED for cpp: no cpp call edge carries call_construct, so gate_named_entry's method rule cannot fire and a C++ method call matches a function-kind POSIX entry."  # dd32
      root_cause: "cpp.py emits meta {evidence_lang, evidence_type} only and never sets call_construct, for either a free-function call or a method call. The F3 gate consumes call_construct to refuse unverified method receivers; with the field absent the refusal is unreachable for the whole language."  # dd32
- op: discuss  # 28f0
  at: '2026-08-17T01:59:34Z'  # 28f0
  by: agent  # 28f0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 28f0
  clock: 2  # 28f0
  nonce: 28f0  # 28f0
  message: "DEFECT CONFIRMED AND FIXED; THIS ITEM'S OWN ROOT_CAUSE AND HEADLINE ARE BOTH\nWRONG, in ways that matter to anyone reading it. 2026-08-16, dev 322a153915.\n\nTHE FILED FALSE POSITIVE IS REAL. Minimal fixture, one translation unit that\ngenuinely includes <sys/wait.h>, positive control passing:\n\n    BEFORE  total_io_edges=2   sys/wait.wait from positive_control   TRUE\n                               sys/wait.wait from the_defect         FALSE\n    AFTER   total_io_edges=1   sys/wait.wait from positive_control   TRUE\n\nCORRECTION 1 -- THE HEADLINE. \"cpp emits call_construct on ZERO call edges\" is\nfalse. Measured on a full whisper.cpp behavior map: 34,983 cpp call edges,\n14,361 carrying call_construct (41.1%). The true statement is narrower and\nworse: the values are function=14,360 and method=1. The single method came from\nthe two-level this->field->method() branch, the only site that stamped it.\nThe distinction is not pedantry -- gate_named_entry falls through on any value\nthat is not literally \"method\", so \"function\" and absent are the SAME value to\nit. A census counting \"edges carrying call_construct\" reports C++ at 41%\ncovered for a gate that sees 0.003%.\n\nCORRECTION 2 -- THE ROOT CAUSE. \"cpp.py ... never sets call_construct\" is false;\nit has stamped it since 2026-05-05, three months before this item was filed.\nRoot cause is that it never stamps \"method\" on the ordinary member call: the\nanalyzer computes is_member_call (it must, to suppress the STL fallback) and\ndiscards it.\n\nCORRECTION 3 -- AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT COSTS A SESSION. \"The fix belongs at\nthe producer\" is false. Stamping the construct in cpp.py is provably INERT for\nthis defect: measured on whisper.cpp, the producer-only change moves ZERO\nboundaries. The filed FP never reaches gate_named_entry at all. cpp.py sets the\nmodule slot to the file's whole #include set, which tests as a usable module\nhint, so lookup_with_module returns from its MODULE-FILTER branch -- which does\nnot read call_construct -- before the gate runs. The gate's own docstring says\n\"gate_named_entry already knows what to do with it the moment it arrives\"; it\nnever arrives.\n\nTHE OBVIOUS CONSUMER FIX IS WRONG, MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. Refusing function-kind\nwhenever call_construct == \"method\" removed 51 matches on whisper.cpp: the 2\ntarget false positives and 49 TRUE ones (os.Open x6, os.Stat x6, fmt.Fprintln\nx6, fmt.Fprintf x5, net/http.NewRequest, logging.exception, os.makedirs,\nos.listdir, ...). Cause: call_construct == \"method\" does NOT mean \"instance\nmethod\". Go's grammar spells a package-qualified function call as a selector\nexpression, identical in shape to an instance call, and the analyzer stamps\nboth:\n\n    os.Open(p)   construct=method   module_slot=os          <- package FUNCTION\n    f.Close()    construct=method   module_slot=external    <- instance METHOD\n\nThat is WHY the F3 gate runs only on the no-module-hint branch: when the slot\nnames a real package it IS the receiver evidence. I shipped this rule, measured\nit, and reverted it. Recorded because the reasoning is seductive and the item\nas filed points straight at it.\n\nTHE FIX THAT LANDED, and the principle it rests on. A DISJUNCTIVE module slot\nis file context, not receiver evidence, so the construct rule applies to it\nexactly as it applies to no hint at all. cpp.py states the semantics itself --\n\"this call could be from any of the included headers\" -- which is an uncertainty\nset. Arity is the discriminator and _module_hint_candidates already computes it:\n\n    os / fmt / net/http / java.io.File            -> 1 candidate    definite\n    sys/wait.h,condition_variable,future,mutex    -> 6 candidates   disjunction\n    stdio.h                                       -> 2 candidates   disjunction\n\nSo: cpp.py stamps method on member calls (dot and arrow), and the module-filter\nbranch drops function-kind candidates when the construct is method AND the slot\nis disjunctive. Go, Python and Java never reach the new branch.\n\nNON-DESTRUCTION MEASURED ON BOTH SIGNS, one counterfactual run per repo\n(every lookup answered twice, with the construct as passed and withheld):\n\n    whisper.cpp   53,996 lookups / 2,379 method-construct-with-hint\n                  REMOVED 2  (both sys/wait.wait -- the filed FPs)  ADDED 0\n    alertmanager  11,357 lookups / 5,086 method-construct-with-hint\n                  REMOVED 0                                          ADDED 0\n\nThe Go zero is the load-bearing control: it is the population the first attempt\ndestroyed 49 of.\n\nWHAT IS NOT CLOSED. A cpp file whose #include set happens to expand to ONE\ncandidate would still take the definite-hint path. I did not measure how many\nsuch files exist. Also untouched: the RESOLVED cpp emission path stamps\n\"function\" unconditionally at two sites, so a member call that resolves to a\nproject symbol is affirmatively MISLABELLED (not merely unlabelled). That has\nno io-boundary consequence today -- a resolved dst is not matched against the\nexternal catalogue -- but it is a false statement in the meta that other\nconsumers read, and it is NOT fixed here.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO THE OTHER TWO ITEMS -- THEY ARE NOT ONE CLASS. This item states\nthat INV-gijis and INV-suril are \"SAME CLASS\". They are two different failure\ndirections through the two branches of one function:\n\n    if call_construct == \"method\": return None        JOB 1, precision:\n                                                      stop a METHOD call from\n                                                      matching a FUNCTION-kind\n                                                      entry.  <- THIS ITEM\n    non_method = [h for h in hits if h.kind != \"method\"]\n                                                      JOB 2, recall:\n                                                      drop METHOD-kind entries\n                                                      when no module hint.\n                                                      <- INV-suril, INV-linub\n\nINV-suril (java Files catalogued kind=method, no hint) fails job 2 and is a\nfalse NEGATIVE -- that is INV-linub's class, and INV-linub's own exposure table\nalready says \"c/cpp: 0% method-kind -- NOT at risk from this class\", which is\ncorrect and is the tell. This item is the opposite sign. A single shared\nproducer-side rule would not have addressed both.\n\nFLEET CENSUS, since the class question asked for it. Analyzers that stamp\ncall_construct but can NEVER stamp \"method\" (so job 1 is unreachable for them):\nbash, clojure, commonlisp, elixir, elm, erlang, fsharp, groovy, haskell, julia,\nkotlin, ocaml -- 12, plus js/ts which never stamps the field at all (INV-gijis).\nRich stampers: go, py, ruby, csharp, rust, dart, java, scala, swift.\nSECOND HOLE, UNFILED AND UNMEASURED: the fleet emits 13 distinct call_construct\nvalues and job 1 tests equality with \"method\" exactly, so go's\ninterface_dispatch and chained_return_type and csharp's method_group -- all\nmethod-construct calls -- fall through the refusal. Mechanism read in code, FP\nvolume NOT measured; stated as a hypothesis, not a finding."  # 28f0
